WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.060 --> 00:00:05.740
Aquaman has netted a gigantic haul at the
box office with audiences and critics enjoying

00:00:05.740 --> 00:00:11.160
the stunning deep-sea adventure, but how does
the half-human half-Atlantean superhero measure

00:00:11.160 --> 00:00:15.620
up against DC's previous critical and commercial
sensation, Wonder Woman?

00:00:15.620 --> 00:00:20.160
Yippee-ki-yay, movie lovers, I'm Jan and in
this video I'm comparing DC's two most recent

00:00:20.160 --> 00:00:23.380
stand alone origin movies for Aquaman and
Wonder Woman.

00:00:23.380 --> 00:00:27.000
Keep watching to the end where I'll reveal
the same animated easter egg that was snuck

00:00:27.000 --> 00:00:28.480
into both movies!

00:00:28.480 --> 00:00:32.009
And if you've got any other upcoming movies
you'd like me to compare, let me know in the

00:00:32.009 --> 00:00:33.009
comments below.

00:00:33.009 --> 00:00:37.520
If there's one thing Aquaman and Wonder Woman
both succeed at, it's the impressive scale

00:00:37.520 --> 00:00:38.620
of their world-building.

00:00:38.620 --> 00:00:43.010
In Aquaman, we get to visit all of the seven
kingdoms by the end of the movie, so there's

00:00:43.010 --> 00:00:45.830
plenty of amazing underwater spectacle.

00:00:45.830 --> 00:00:49.960
What's great about Aquaman is that unlike
some of DC's previous cinematic releases,

00:00:49.960 --> 00:00:53.600
it feels like director James Wan has been
given much more free reign to indulge his

00:00:53.600 --> 00:00:56.500
imagination without too much studio meddling.

00:00:56.500 --> 00:01:00.260
And the scale of the world building makes
me think of epic fantasy flicks like Lord

00:01:00.260 --> 00:01:05.519
of The Rings, which is fitting as, according
to EW, Wan "considers Aquaman a fantasy film,

00:01:05.519 --> 00:01:06.670
not a superhero movie".

00:01:06.670 --> 00:01:11.890
So, when we enter Atlantis for the first time,
the visuals are simply breath-taking – gotta

00:01:11.890 --> 00:01:16.270
give props for that to the film's production
designer, art department, and VFX teams.

00:01:16.270 --> 00:01:20.749
There are plenty of arresting sights throughout
the movie, and just when you think it can't

00:01:20.749 --> 00:01:23.000
get any more insane, it does!

00:01:23.000 --> 00:01:27.359
It's like a weird fever dream of a film, with
so much to look at on screen, that at times

00:01:27.359 --> 00:01:29.920
you feel you can't quite take it all in.

00:01:29.920 --> 00:01:34.720
While the movie clearly lays out its own individual
world, it's impossible not to make some comparisons

00:01:34.720 --> 00:01:40.359
with other fantasy and sci-fi worlds like
Star Wars, which Wan cites as a major influence,

00:01:40.359 --> 00:01:44.921
and Atlantis's gateway bridge and border control
remind me of Asgard's Bifrost Bridge in the

00:01:44.921 --> 00:01:46.170
Thor movies.

00:01:46.170 --> 00:01:50.360
And the light, colourful tone of Aquaman isn't
miles away from Thor: Ragnarok.

00:01:50.360 --> 00:01:54.789
The extensive travels we go on in Aquaman
mean we get quite a mix of genres, which I'll

00:01:54.789 --> 00:01:59.520
talk about shortly, and they also set up some
cool moments like the multiple-wall-smashing

00:01:59.520 --> 00:02:04.700
action in Sicily, and the monstrous, portrait-like
shot of Arthur and Mera diving into the Trench

00:02:04.700 --> 00:02:06.209
with a lit flare.

00:02:06.209 --> 00:02:10.259
And then there's the crazy hidden kingdom
full of dinosaurs at the centre of the Earth

00:02:10.259 --> 00:02:14.819
which, although inspired by comics, in the
film feels almost like Aquaman has briefly

00:02:14.819 --> 00:02:18.590
crossed over time and space and landed in
Jurassic Park.

00:02:18.590 --> 00:02:23.099
Wonder Woman's world-building is, let's be
honest, a good bit more restrained than Aquaman's.

00:02:23.099 --> 00:02:27.400
There's a lovely introduction to Themyscira
and the Amazons during the scenes where we

00:02:27.400 --> 00:02:32.159
watch Diana grow up and learn about the customs
of her warrior people, though we do also get

00:02:32.159 --> 00:02:36.550
some obvious green-screen moments such as
young Diana jumping off a wall.

00:02:36.550 --> 00:02:41.540
And Aquaman also suffers from some poor CGI
during flashbacks including the weird de-aging

00:02:41.540 --> 00:02:44.780
of Arthur's father and Willem Dafoe's Vulko.

00:02:44.780 --> 00:02:48.829
In some ways, it's a shame the Wonder Woman
movie doesn't spend longer on the paradise

00:02:48.829 --> 00:02:53.270
island, but the story, of course, needs Diana
to leave and defeat Ares, the God of War.

00:02:53.270 --> 00:02:58.159
Though the movie's design and art teams certainly
do a marvellous job creating Diana's worlds,

00:02:58.159 --> 00:03:02.599
the locations we visit in Wonder Woman are,
necessarily, more grounded in reality and

00:03:02.599 --> 00:03:05.650
their range isn't quite as rich as in Aquaman.

00:03:05.650 --> 00:03:09.690
Where Wonder Woman did things better is in
exploring the mythology of Themyscira and

00:03:09.690 --> 00:03:14.450
the Amazons, rather than Aquaman's somewhat
brief flashback to the formerly above-water

00:03:14.450 --> 00:03:16.290
world of Atlantis.

00:03:16.290 --> 00:03:20.879
Aquaman is a real whirlwind of genres; it's
a bit like the filmmakers decided to throw

00:03:20.879 --> 00:03:22.959
everything at the wall and see what sticks.

00:03:22.959 --> 00:03:27.849
There are moments that lean heavily into horror,
which isn't surprising given that the co-creator

00:03:27.849 --> 00:03:31.010
of the Saw and Conjuring franchises is in
the director's chair.

00:03:31.010 --> 00:03:35.350
There are action-adventure sequences in the
vein of Indiana Jones, The Mummy movies, and

00:03:35.350 --> 00:03:39.930
Romancing the Stone, with a touch of the Fast
&amp; Furious franchise thrown in – remember

00:03:39.930 --> 00:03:43.400
that James Wan directed the seventh film in
that series.

00:03:43.400 --> 00:03:48.349
And the influence of creature features also
rears its ginormous head; in fact, Wan has

00:03:48.349 --> 00:03:51.299
talked of Aquaman being a horror-monster movie.

00:03:51.299 --> 00:03:55.510
On top of that, there's a ton of Star Wars-esque
moments; as well as fantastical children's

00:03:55.510 --> 00:03:58.859
stories and Arthurian legends thrown into
the blend.

00:03:58.859 --> 00:04:03.799
Some of these inspirations gel together better
than others; personally, I loved the horror-heavy

00:04:03.799 --> 00:04:05.350
Trench sequence.

00:04:05.350 --> 00:04:09.110
When it comes to Aquaman's tone though, you
could certainly never accuse the movie of

00:04:09.110 --> 00:04:14.079
taking itself too seriously, which feels right
given the character's history.

00:04:14.079 --> 00:04:18.010
Director Wan isn't afraid to pile on the cheese
in an all-out effort to capture the kind of

00:04:18.010 --> 00:04:21.030
humorous moments found in old-school action
flicks.

00:04:21.030 --> 00:04:25.680
So, as well as bucketfuls of action and a
less-than-subtle ecological message, we also

00:04:25.680 --> 00:04:29.569
get a boat-load of jokes which, depending
on what makes you laugh, you'll either find

00:04:29.569 --> 00:04:33.000
funny, groan-worthy, or a mix of both.

00:04:33.000 --> 00:04:35.180
"Permission to come aboard."

00:04:35.180 --> 00:04:39.750
For me, the humour in Wonder Woman manages
to find its target and lands more often than

00:04:39.750 --> 00:04:42.860
not, giving it an overall better hit rate
than Aquaman.

00:04:42.860 --> 00:04:46.900
The interaction between Diana, who's never
seen a man before, and Steve Trevor leads

00:04:46.900 --> 00:04:52.270
to some truly funny moments, as does Diana's
innocence when it comes to life outside Themyscira

00:04:52.270 --> 00:04:54.740
and how she adjusts to the new world.

00:04:54.740 --> 00:04:59.840
Like Aquaman, Wonder Woman also blends a variety
of genres, taking us from swords and sandals

00:04:59.840 --> 00:05:03.910
territory, through screwball comedy, to a
war drama with added elements of spy thriller,

00:05:03.910 --> 00:05:07.250
and finishing with the obligatory big superhero
battle.

00:05:07.250 --> 00:05:10.780
Compared to how well so much of the rest of
the movie works, thanks in no small part to

00:05:10.780 --> 00:05:15.110
compelling performances from the lead and
supporting cast, the finale feels more generic,

00:05:15.110 --> 00:05:17.550
even if it does give us some memorable moments.

00:05:17.550 --> 00:05:22.400
As for its villains, Wonder Woman isn't as
wondrous as it could be since its baddies

00:05:22.400 --> 00:05:25.830
are drawn rather broadly so not especially
compelling.

00:05:25.830 --> 00:05:30.900
Danny Huston's Ludendorff is largely forgettable
while Elena Anaya makes a bit more of an impression

00:05:30.900 --> 00:05:33.789
as Dr Maru, aka Doctor Poison.

00:05:33.789 --> 00:05:38.389
Given the story's twists and turns, David
Thewlis gets a few more shades to play with,

00:05:38.389 --> 00:05:42.630
though ultimately, we're still left with a
pretty typical CGI-filled end battle.

00:05:42.630 --> 00:05:47.790
In Aquaman, the villainy is really personal
from the get-go – the main antagonist is

00:05:47.790 --> 00:05:52.580
Arthur's half-brother Orm, and the movie's
secondary antagonist, David Kane, aka Black

00:05:52.580 --> 00:05:56.440
Manta, also has a personal, family-based beef
with Aquaman.

00:05:56.440 --> 00:06:00.949
Patrick Wilson's Orm gets more screen time
then the individual villains in Wonder Woman,

00:06:00.949 --> 00:06:05.900
so in theory he gets the opportunity to make
more of an impression, but in reality, like

00:06:05.900 --> 00:06:09.740
many of the characters in Aquaman, Orm has
a heck of a lot of not-very-scintillating

00:06:09.740 --> 00:06:12.419
exposition to deliver in his dialogue.

00:06:12.419 --> 00:06:17.330
Still, opposite Jason Momoa's hard-drinking,
tough but chill vibe as Aquaman, the fact

00:06:17.330 --> 00:06:21.610
that Wilson plays Orm with all the gravitas
and self-importance of a character straight

00:06:21.610 --> 00:06:24.330
out of a Shakespeare drama seems strangely
fitting.

00:06:24.330 --> 00:06:29.199
As for Black Manta, we only get so much of
him and his comic-book accurate costume this

00:06:29.199 --> 00:06:35.789
time as he's basically there to be introduced
and built up as an important future foe.

00:06:35.789 --> 00:06:40.020
The Aquaman movie certainly doesn't skimp
on fights or battle scenes, showing us a vast

00:06:40.020 --> 00:06:45.360
range of confrontations both below water and
on land, including submarines taken over by

00:06:45.360 --> 00:06:52.130
pirates, gladiatorial-style challenges featuring
a drum-playing octopus, weaponised wine bottles,

00:06:52.130 --> 00:06:56.690
storm-tossed ships and deep-sea dives, as
well as a humongous sea-monster voiced by

00:06:56.690 --> 00:07:02.160
Julie Andrews, and that's all before we even
get to the final big underwater battle!

00:07:02.160 --> 00:07:06.669
During that climactic clash of the undersea
titans, we hear a character say 'we are going

00:07:06.669 --> 00:07:10.550
to give them a fight they will never forget',
which feels like a lot of pressure to put

00:07:10.550 --> 00:07:15.000
on the movie's finale, but there are definitely
some extremely eye-popping visuals in this

00:07:15.000 --> 00:07:16.000
sequence.

00:07:16.000 --> 00:07:20.419
There's Arthur rising up out of the ocean
floor astride the Karathen, Orm riding a mosasaur

00:07:20.419 --> 00:07:25.110
that chomps up a colossal crustacean, and
then there's an army of laser-shooting sharks,

00:07:25.110 --> 00:07:28.099
plus a huge array of other crazy creatures.

00:07:28.099 --> 00:07:33.530
Basically, Aquaman doesn't just embrace excess,
it positively revels in it.

00:07:33.530 --> 00:07:37.750
It's like the film is asking the question,
why have less when you can have more, more,

00:07:37.750 --> 00:07:38.750
more?!

00:07:38.750 --> 00:07:42.590
As much as fun as this is, it still falls
into the same trap of many such films by going

00:07:42.590 --> 00:07:46.919
on a touch too long in the final stretch,
especially as the scenes are almost entirely

00:07:46.919 --> 00:07:51.490
CGI and the longer that's on screen, the more
it starts to look noticeable.

00:07:51.490 --> 00:07:56.699
Sadly, Wonder Woman is also guilty of that
overly-long final CGI showdown with some ropey

00:07:56.699 --> 00:07:59.220
VFX when Diana goes up against Ares.

00:07:59.220 --> 00:08:03.259
However, there are some really cool fight
scenes in Wonder Woman that are especially

00:08:03.259 --> 00:08:05.389
memorable for their dramatic settings.

00:08:05.389 --> 00:08:09.360
The Superman-inspired alleyway fight where
Diana saves Steve with her bracelets is a

00:08:09.360 --> 00:08:10.370
delightful scene.

00:08:10.370 --> 00:08:15.699
And there are some beautiful balletic, martial-arts-style
moments in the battle between the German soldiers

00:08:15.699 --> 00:08:18.900
and the Amazons, even though some of the effects
may be flawed.

00:08:18.900 --> 00:08:24.259
But Diana striding out on to No Man's Land
is the movie's stand-out action scene.

00:08:24.259 --> 00:08:29.360
Nothing in Aquaman is quite as visceral, moving,
and awe-inspiring as that moment.

00:08:29.360 --> 00:08:33.850
And the absolutely, mind-blowingly amazing
thing about that scene is that it was very

00:08:33.850 --> 00:08:37.940
nearly removed from the movie as initially
the studio executives couldn't get their heads

00:08:37.940 --> 00:08:42.479
around who Diana was meant to be fighting
in the scene as it's not about just one specific

00:08:42.479 --> 00:08:43.479
big bad.

00:08:43.479 --> 00:08:48.200
Now, you know I love my superhero movies,
but one thing about them I'm not so keen on,

00:08:48.200 --> 00:08:53.570
is their romantic subplots because they often
feel shoehorned into the story, and the romantic

00:08:53.570 --> 00:08:58.000
interests frequently have less-than-interesting
roles with not a lot to do other than be saved

00:08:58.000 --> 00:09:00.740
by and swoon over the heroic lead.

00:09:00.740 --> 00:09:05.040
While Amber Heard's Mera does get some cool
moments in the Aquaman movie, holding back

00:09:05.040 --> 00:09:09.760
waves and kicking some ass, a big part of
her role is to relay information to Aquaman/Arthur,

00:09:09.760 --> 00:09:12.680
and by extension, to us in the audience.

00:09:12.680 --> 00:09:17.180
Although Mera's engaged to Arthur's half-brother
Orm, most of her scenes are alongside Jason

00:09:17.180 --> 00:09:18.180
Momoa.

00:09:18.180 --> 00:09:22.990
So, it's unfortunate that Momoa and Heard's
on-screen chemistry isn't exactly sizzling.

00:09:22.990 --> 00:09:27.140
It's not like there's zero spark between them,
it's more that their eventual kiss doesn't

00:09:27.140 --> 00:09:32.160
really feel earned but rather simply inevitable
because that's what happens in scripts like

00:09:32.160 --> 00:09:33.160
this.

00:09:33.160 --> 00:09:37.011
And that's why the way their relationship's
depicted can't compare to Diana and Steve

00:09:37.011 --> 00:09:38.260
in Wonder Woman.

00:09:38.260 --> 00:09:42.860
Thanks to Gal Gadot and Chris Pine's line
delivery and obvious chemistry, Patty Jenkins'

00:09:42.860 --> 00:09:47.700
direction, and Allan Heinberg's script, Wonder
Woman takes small, quiet moments like Diana

00:09:47.700 --> 00:09:52.440
and Steve's boat journey or dancing together
after saving a war-torn village and turns

00:09:52.440 --> 00:09:56.600
them into humorous or touching scenes that
reveal a lot to us about them as individuals

00:09:56.600 --> 00:09:57.860
and potential partners.

00:09:57.860 --> 00:10:02.510
So, when Steve tells Diana he loves her in
the final act, it's not a throwaway moment

00:10:02.510 --> 00:10:06.740
that's inevitable because that's what these
movies do, instead the script has worked to

00:10:06.740 --> 00:10:10.750
build their characters to this point and earned
that moment.

00:10:10.750 --> 00:10:15.990
Much like her fellow sea-based red-head, Ariel
in The Little Mermaid, Mera gets several literal

00:10:15.990 --> 00:10:21.370
fish out of water moments where she's faced
with the strange, new world of the surface-dwellers.

00:10:21.370 --> 00:10:25.600
Curiously, Diana has her own Ariel moment
in Wonder Woman when she drags Steve Trevor

00:10:25.600 --> 00:10:30.029
from the sea in a scene that clearly pays
homage to Disney's animated feature.

00:10:30.029 --> 00:10:34.779
OK, Now it's your turn to decide whether you
think Aquaman or Wonder Woman is the better

00:10:34.779 --> 00:10:35.779
movie!

00:10:35.779 --> 00:10:39.260
I've added a poll where you can vote – just
tap in the top right of this video and after

00:10:39.260 --> 00:10:41.520
you've cast your vote you'll be able to see
the results.

00:10:41.520 --> 00:10:45.170
Let me know what you think in the comments
below, and if you enjoyed this why not hit

